.Dd September 14, 2026
.Dt AUTHAGENTD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm authagentd
.Nd session-mint boundary broker and elevation authenticator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon is the identity-to-capability mint boundary of the capability system.
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.authagent
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel.
Its job is to turn the fact that
.Dq an authenticator has verified a login for uid Ar U
into a minted lookup channel scoped to that session and carrying that
principal's anointment set, and, on request from a process inside a
session, to extend that set by one name for one command
.Pq elevation, the Xr sudo 8 and Xr doas 1 replacement .
.Pp
An authenticator that has established a login for a principal
.Pq Xr login 1 , Xr su 1 , Xr sshd 8
connects to
.Dq Li system.authagent
and asks for that session's capability bundle.
.Nm
mints the scoped session lookup channel over its own bootstrap channel to
.Xr switchboard 8
and forwards it to the authenticator, which installs it for the new session.
The authenticator never holds mint authority itself, and no plaintext
credential from the login program is ever trusted as authority.
.Pp
.Nm
is launched and supervised by
.Xr switchboard 8
from the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/AuthAgent.cap
bundle; it is not started by
.Xr init 8 ,
and it is not intended to be run from the command line.
.Ss Authoritative principal resolution
.Nm
does not trust the principal attributes supplied on the wire.
A mint request carries only a uid; an elevation request carries none, its
uid being the kernel-stamped sender credential of the message.
Before entering its sandbox,
.Nm
obtains read-only descriptors for
.Pa /etc/passwd ,
.Pa /etc/group
and
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
from
.Xr tzfsd 8 ,
then enters capability mode with
.Xr cap_enter 2 .
For each request it resolves the uid to its
.Xr passwd 5
entry and full group membership
.Pq its primary group plus every group whose member list names it
itself, so a compromised login client cannot claim membership it does not
have.
.Ss Principal policy
The resolved principal is matched against
.Pa /Capabilities/Config/principal-policy.ucl ,
which decides what the session holds.
The file is a
.Dq Li principals
block of entries matched in file order; the first entry whose
.Cm uids
or
.Cm groups
list matches the principal wins, and an entry with neither list
.Pq conventionally Dq default
is the fallback.
Each entry has three knobs:
.Bl -tag -width "admin_rights"
.It Cm anointments
The names the session holds from login, silently, for every process under
it.
An empty list is not
.Dq connects to nothing :
every endpoint a provider publishes without a requirement stays reachable.
.It Cm may_elevate
The names the principal does not hold but may ask for one command at a
time through
.Xr anoint 1 ,
after re-entering its password.
Absent or empty means the principal can never elevate.
.It Cm admin_rights
Whether connections from the session carry the
.Dv SERVICE_RIGHTS_ADMIN
bit that providers honour as an in-endpoint bypass.
It is separate from reach.
It defaults to true only for an entry whose anointments are
.Dq Li * .
.El
.Pp
.Dq Li *
is legal in
.Cm anointments
and
.Cm may_elevate
in this file and nowhere else.
The session kind is SYSTEM
.Pq full discovery
when the grant holds
.Dq Li *
or carries admin rights, and a per-uid USER channel carrying the listed set
otherwise.
The installed default grants
.Dq Li *
with admin rights to uid 0 and members of group
.Dq wheel ,
and nothing to everyone else; it is a configuration file, so local changes
are preserved across package upgrades.
The legacy top-level
.Ic admin { uids; groups; }
form is still accepted and means the same.
.Pp
The
.Dq capability
user is not a principal: it is the uid
.Xr switchboard 8
runs units as, and units take their set from their own policy file.
.Nm
logs a warning at startup if the policy grants that uid anything.
When the policy is missing or malformed
.Nm
falls back to the historical rule
.Pq uid 0 or a member of group Dq wheel holds everything with admin rights
and logs the fallback so it is observable.
Every mint logs at
.Dv LOG_INFO
the kind and the shape of the grant applied.
.Ss Caller gate for minting
Minting is gated on a held right, not on a name.
Only a caller holding
.Dv SERVICE_RIGHTS_ADMIN
on its channel may request a mint.
.Xr switchboard 8
stamps that right on a brokered session only for an ambient login-session
lookup over a SYSTEM (full-discovery) channel, which is exactly and only the
channel the login family
.Pq Xr login 1 , Xr su 1 , Xr sshd 8
reaches
.Nm
over.
Every ordinary unit, and every login session reaching
.Nm
for elevation, is stamped without the admin bit and is refused a mint with
.Er EPERM
before the request is parsed or any channel is minted.
This closes the proxy escalation in which a service or a user would
otherwise mint itself an admin session.
.Pp
The minted leaf channel is re-attenuated to
.Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE
before it is sent, so the reply's own descriptor pass consumes it to
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE
at the login program: the session leaf receives a non-re-delegable lookup
channel.
A forwarding authenticator (an
.Xr sshd 8
monitor) may instead request a still-transferable channel and re-attenuate it
before its single forward.
.Ss Elevation
The
.Dv AUTHAGENT_OP_ELEVATE
operation, exposed to programs as
.Fn service_elevate
and to the shell as
.Xr anoint 1 ,
asks for a lookup channel holding the caller's session set plus one named
anointment.
.Nm
is resolvable from every session so this request can reach it; the mint
gate above keeps that opening limited to elevation.
The request is processed in this order, each refusal audited to
.Dv LOG_AUTHPRIV :
.Bl -enum
.It
The caller's connection must carry the login-session label.
A unit's channel is refused
.Er EPERM :
units declare their anointments, they do not elevate.
.It
The uid is taken from the kernel-stamped sender of the message, never from
the payload, which carries only the requested name and a password.
A malformed request
.Pq wrong size, version or flags, an unterminated field, an attached descriptor, or a name that is not a reverse-domain name
is
.Er EINVAL .
.It
The name must be in the principal's
.Cm may_elevate
.Pq or the entry must list Dq Li * ,
else
.Er EPERM
before any password is examined.
.It
Repeated failures are rate-limited per uid: after five failures within
sixty seconds the request is refused
.Er EAGAIN
without a password check until the window has elapsed; a success clears
the counter.
.It
The password is verified inside the sandbox with
.Xr crypt 3
against the hash in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd ,
read through the retained descriptor and zeroed after each check, using a
constant-time comparison.
A mismatch is
.Er EACCES ;
an account with an empty or locked hash is
.Er EPERM ;
a uid with no record is
.Er ENOENT .
PAM is not used: module stacks cannot run in the sandbox, and
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr su 1
and
.Xr sshd 8
keep their own PAM.
If
.Xr tzfsd 8
did not grant
.Pa /etc/master.passwd ,
elevation is disabled and answers
.Er ENXIO ;
minting is unaffected.
.It
On success a channel of the same kind and uid is minted whose set is the
principal's policy set plus the requested name
.Pq Er E2BIG if the set is already full ,
attenuated to
.Dv CAP_XFER_ONCE ,
and returned.
The grant is logged at
.Dv LOG_NOTICE .
.El
.Pp
The request buffer holding the password is zeroed on both sides after use.
Nothing is cached: every elevation authenticates again.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /Capabilities/Config/principal-policy.ucl" -compact
.It Pa /Capabilities/Config/principal-policy.ucl
principal policy: anointments, may_elevate, admin_rights per principal
.It Pa /etc/master.passwd
password hashes for in-agent elevation authentication (read-only, granted
by the filesystem daemon's open policy)
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/AuthAgent.cap
capability bundle shipping
.Nm
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr anoint 1 ,
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr crypt 3 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr capsule 8 ,
.Xr sshd 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8 ,
.Xr tzfsd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon appeared in
.Fx 5BSD
as the session-mint boundary between authenticators and the capability
service world.
Elevation was added with IPC anointments.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
